[patterns/application/api-microservices/PTN-APP-API-019-input-validation-pattern.md#problem-4-path-traversal-in-file-upload-apis]

#### Problem 4: Path Traversal in File Upload APIs

File upload endpoints accepting user-supplied filenames without validation enable path traversal attacks accessing files outside intended directories.

**Real-World Example - Document Upload API (December 2024):**
```csharp
// VULNERABLE: Unsanitized filename in file path construction
[HttpPost("upload")]
public async Task<IActionResult> UploadDocument(IFormFile file, string documentType)
{
    if (file == null) return BadRequest("No file provided");

    // documentType from user input, not validated
    string uploadPath = $"/app/uploads/{documentType}/{file.FileName}";

    using (var stream = new FileStream(uploadPath, FileMode.Create))
    {
        await file.CopyToAsync(stream);
    }

    return Ok(new { path = uploadPath });
}

// ATTACK: Malicious client uploads file with path traversal:
// POST /api/documents/upload
// documentType=../../etc&filename=passwd

// RESULTING FILE PATH:
// /app/uploads/../../etc/passwd
// Overwrites /app/etc/passwd file (command injection opportunity)
```

**Impact**: Remote code execution via overwritten configuration files, full system compromise, emergency system rebuild.

**Root Cause**:
- No validation of `documentType` parameter
- No filename sanitization (allowing ../ path traversal)
- File path constructed via string concatenation

